INT. FLOODED COUNTY COURTHOUSE - MAKESHIFT COURTROOM - DAY

Sandbags by the doors. Folding tables. A generator hums.

JUDGE OLUWASEUN ADEYEMI, 60s, presides from a card table. DISTRICT ATTORNEY RHONDA KESSLER, 40s, stands with a soggy file. PUBLIC DEFENDER MARCUS TIBBS, 30s, trouser cuffs wet. The defendant, WALTER "SKEETER" DUVAL, 50s, sits in rubber boots. COURT CLERK PENNY FARRELL, 20s, types on a laptop on a milk crate. BAILIFF GUS OKAFOR, 50s, leans on the doorframe.

JUDGE ADEYEMI
Be seated. We'll proceed on the record, such as it is.

RHONDA
Your Honor, the State requests a continuance. Half our exhibits are under four feet of river.

MARCUS
Defense objects. My client has waited eleven months. He can't wait another flood season.

JUDGE ADEYEMI
Mr. Tibbs, your client is charged with stealing a boat.

MARCUS
Borrowing, Your Honor. And that boat is the only reason forty people made it off Pratt Street last night.

RHONDA
Forty people who would not have needed rescuing if he hadn't taken the boat from the marina in the first place.

MARCUS
That is a logical pretzel.

SKEETER
Can I say something?

MARCUS
No.

JUDGE ADEYEMI
Let him.

SKEETER
I'd do it again, Judge. Ain't proud, ain't sorry.

RHONDA
Your Honor, that's practically a confession.

JUDGE ADEYEMI
It's practically a sermon. Ms. Kessler, do you still wish to prosecute?

RHONDA
(after a beat)
The State will reduce to trespass.

MARCUS
Time served.

JUDGE ADEYEMI
Time served. Mr. Duval, return the boat with a thank-you note.

SKEETER
Yes, ma'am.

JUDGE ADEYEMI
Court is adjourned. Mind the step.
